  "worked_solution": "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(x) & =(x)^{\u2032}=1, \\\\ {y}^{\u2033}(x) & =(1)^{\u2032}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-6x(1)+10(x)=4x.\\,\\,\u2713\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{\u2032}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{\u2033}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{\u2032}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(6x)-6x(3{x}^{2})+10({x}^{3})=4x.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{\u00a0and\u00a0}B$ are arbitrary constants.",
